* startup  --  OS-9/68K freeware collection** This is a Microware-shell script.  It runs if you start os9exec with* Microware's `shell'; the public-domain bash on this disk does NOT read it.** Recommended: keep your own OS-9 system as /dd and mount this collection* as /h0, so the hardcoded /h0 paths in many of these programs resolve:**     OS9DISK=<your disk>  OS9H0=<this image, named h0>  os9exec shell** With no OS-9 of your own, run this disk by itself using its own bash and* name programs by their full path (see the readme at the disk root):**     OS9DISK=<this disk>  os9exec /dd/CMDS/bash** DOC/README-RUNNING explains all three arrangements.*setenv PATH /dd/CMDS:/dd/CMDS/GAMES:/h1/CMDSsetenv TERM vt100setenv TMACDIR /dd/LIBsetenv HELPDIR /dd/SYS/HELPsetenv SIMPATH /dd/SBPROLOG/MODLIBsetenv PEP /dd/SYS/PEP** TMACDIR  nroff's macro directory -- without it `nroff -man' fails with*          "unable to open macro file /tmac.an", which looks like a missing*          file and is not.  48 man pages under /dd/DOC become readable.* HELPDIR  the OS-9 help system -- `help tar', `help indent', 12 topics.* SIMPATH  SB-Prolog's module library.  Without it wam.sbprolog starts, runs,*          prints nothing and exits, which looks exactly like a broken build.* PEP      pep's character-set tables.  pep searches the name as given and*          then $PEP/<name>, and nowhere else -- so without it `pep -g' and*          the -i/-m/-d conversions stop with "can't open translation table".** Put your own start-up commands below.** ------------------------------------------------------------------------* Bringing your OWN /dd with Microware modules?  Preload the shared tools so* they run from any execution directory -- F$Load searches only the exec dir,* but a module already resident is found from anywhere:**   load cio csl math r68 l68 o68*   load cc cpp c68** The 92 programs starred in DOC/INDEX need cio.  They are NOT in a* separate directory -- if you have a licensed SDK you already have cio, and* keeping 92 good programs apart from the rest is just friction.